INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A ring frame''s traveller number is being selected for a new count. The relationship between traveller weight and yarn count follows an approximate rule: heavier travellers for coarser counts, lighter travellers for finer counts. If a spinner mistakenly uses a HEAVIER-than-recommended traveller for a FINE count (e.g., 60s Ne), what specific failure mode is most likely?', '["No effect at all; traveller weight selection is purely a cosmetic choice with zero relationship to actual spinning performance at any count","A too-heavy traveller for a fine, delicate yarn generates EXCESSIVE tension relative to the yarn''s lower strength (fine yarns have fewer fibers in cross-section and thus lower absolute strength), causing elevated end breakage — the traveller-to-count matching exists specifically to balance tension against the yarn''s actual strength capacity at that count","A too-heavy traveller always REDUCES tension regardless of count, making this scenario''s premise about excessive tension technically backwards","Traveller weight only affects twist insertion rate, with zero relationship to yarn tension or end breakage at any count"]'::jsonb, 'A too-heavy traveller for a fine, delicate yarn generates EXCESSIVE tension relative to the yarn''s lower strength (fine yarns have fewer fibers in cross-section and thus lower absolute strength), causing elevated end breakage — the traveller-to-count matching exists specifically to balance tension against the yarn''s actual strength capacity at that count', 'This directly extends the earlier traveller-weight/tension-control discussion — since traveller weight/drag directly sets yarn balloon tension, and finer counts have fewer fibers in cross-section (lower absolute breaking strength, as established in the spinning-limit discussion), using a traveller weight calibrated for a COARSER count on a FINE count yarn creates tension that exceeds the fine yarn''s actual strength capacity, directly causing elevated end breakage — correct traveller selection must be matched to the SPECIFIC count being spun, not treated as a one-size-fits-all machine setting.', 3, '{"traveller-count-matching"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A spinning mill''s technical team is debugging a chronic problem: yarn from ONE specific ring frame consistently shows higher CV% than identical machines running the identical count/roving. A vibration analysis reveals this specific machine''s spindle bearings show elevated vibration amplitude compared to the other machines. How would elevated spindle vibration plausibly translate into worse yarn evenness (CV%)?', '["Spindle vibration has no physical mechanism by which it could affect yarn mass evenness, making this correlation coincidental with no causal explanation","Vibration can cause micro-variations in the effective twist insertion and yarn tension DURING winding onto the bobbin, and can also affect drafting zone stability upstream if transmitted through the machine frame — both mechanisms can introduce small but real periodic mass/tension irregularities into the yarn, translating mechanical vibration into measurable evenness (CV%) degradation","Vibration only affects the machine''s noise level and energy consumption, with zero possible pathway to affecting yarn mass distribution","Elevated vibration always IMPROVES yarn evenness by helping distribute fiber more randomly, the opposite of the observed correlation in this troubleshooting scenario"]'::jsonb, 'Vibration can cause micro-variations in the effective twist insertion and yarn tension DURING winding onto the bobbin, and can also affect drafting zone stability upstream if transmitted through the machine frame — both mechanisms can introduce small but real periodic mass/tension irregularities into the yarn, translating mechanical vibration into measurable evenness (CV%) degradation', 'This is an advanced mechanical-root-cause diagnostic insight — bearing vibration can propagate through the machine structure to affect the precision of drafting roller spacing/pressure consistency (upstream) and twist/tension uniformity during winding (downstream), both of which are physical mechanisms capable of introducing small, periodic irregularities into yarn mass distribution — recognizing this connection (a MACHINE mechanical condition issue manifesting as a YARN QUALITY metric problem) is exactly the kind of cross-domain diagnostic reasoning a senior spinning technologist needs, rather than assuming CV% problems only ever trace to fiber, roving, or drafting settings.', 3, '{"vibration-CV-diagnosis"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A ring-spinning mill notices that switching cotton varieties (identical micronaire and length, but from a DIFFERENT growing region) causes a measurable INCREASE in end breakage, even though all standard fiber test parameters (micronaire, length, strength, elongation) report as essentially IDENTICAL between the two varieties. What ADDITIONAL, less commonly tested fiber property could plausibly explain this discrepancy?', '["No such explanation is possible; if all standard test parameters are identical, end breakage rate must also be identical, making this scenario contradictory","Fiber-to-fiber FRICTION/COHESION characteristics (surface properties affecting how well fibers grip each other via twist, sometimes influenced by fiber surface wax content, convolutions/crimp structure, or maturity distribution details not fully captured by average micronaire) — since yarn strength depends on both individual fiber strength AND how well twist can convert fiber-to-fiber friction into cohesive yarn strength, two fiber lots with identical AVERAGE standard properties can still behave differently in spinning if their surface/frictional characteristics differ","Fiber color is the only possible explanation, since color is the sole fiber property not captured by standard testing","Growing region alone directly causes spinning performance differences through no identifiable physical fiber property mechanism, making this an unexplainable phenomenon"]'::jsonb, 'Fiber-to-fiber FRICTION/COHESION characteristics (surface properties affecting how well fibers grip each other via twist, sometimes influenced by fiber surface wax content, convolutions/crimp structure, or maturity distribution details not fully captured by average micronaire) — since yarn strength depends on both individual fiber strength AND how well twist can convert fiber-to-fiber friction into cohesive yarn strength, two fiber lots with identical AVERAGE standard properties can still behave differently in spinning if their surface/frictional characteristics differ', 'This is a genuinely advanced insight highlighting that STANDARD HVI-type testing (micronaire, length, strength, elongation) doesn''t capture EVERY fiber property relevant to spinning performance — fiber surface characteristics (natural wax content, convolution/crimp structure affecting inter-fiber friction, and the DISTRIBUTION not just average of maturity) directly affect how effectively twist can convert individual fiber strength into cohesive yarn strength, so two cotton lots with identical standard test averages can still show genuinely different real-world spinning performance if these less-commonly-tested properties differ, illustrating the limits of relying purely on standard HVI parameters for predicting spinnability.', 3, '{"fiber-friction-spinnability"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A rotor spinning machine''s rotor diameter is being selected for a new yarn count/application. SMALLER rotor diameters generally enable HIGHER rotor speeds (and thus higher production rates), but also tend to produce yarn with a MORE compact, twisted structure closer to ring-spun character. Why does smaller rotor diameter push yarn structure toward more ring-like character?', '["Rotor diameter has no relationship to yarn structure; only rotor speed affects yarn character, making this premise technically incorrect","A smaller rotor means fiber travels a shorter path and experiences a tighter radius of curvature during the fiber-collection and twist-insertion process, which tends to produce a more tightly, uniformly twisted yarn structure with fewer loosely-wrapped surface fibers — closer to the compact structure of ring-spun yarn — compared to a larger rotor''s longer collection path and looser resulting structure","Larger rotor diameter always produces MORE ring-like yarn structure, the opposite of the actual relationship between rotor size and yarn structure character","Rotor diameter can only be changed by completely replacing the entire spinning machine, making diameter-based yarn structure tuning technically impractical in any real mill"]'::jsonb, 'A smaller rotor means fiber travels a shorter path and experiences a tighter radius of curvature during the fiber-collection and twist-insertion process, which tends to produce a more tightly, uniformly twisted yarn structure with fewer loosely-wrapped surface fibers — closer to the compact structure of ring-spun yarn — compared to a larger rotor''s longer collection path and looser resulting structure', 'This extends the earlier ring-vs-rotor yarn structure discussion with a rotor-specific engineering detail — a smaller rotor diameter changes the geometry of the fiber collection groove and twist-insertion process (tighter radius, shorter fiber path), which tends to produce a more tightly and uniformly twisted, more compact yarn structure (fewer of the loosely-wrapped surface ''wrapper fibers'' characteristic of rotor yarn) — this is why modern smaller-diameter rotor spinning technology has progressively closed some of the historical quality gap between rotor and ring-spun yarn character, alongside the separate, simultaneous benefit of enabling higher rotor speeds and production rates.', 3, '{"rotor-diameter-yarn-structure"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A spinning mill''s cost analysis compares ring spinning vs air-jet spinning for a specific 100% cotton, 30s Ne combed yarn intended for premium woven shirting. Air-jet spinning offers dramatically higher production speed, but the mill ultimately chooses RING spinning for this specific product. What is the most likely technical justification?', '["Air-jet spinning is always technically incapable of processing cotton fiber at all, making this comparison scenario impossible","For premium woven shirting requiring the smooth, compact, high-strength, low-hairiness yarn character that ring spinning''s mechanism specifically produces, air-jet spinning''s yarn structure (which, despite high speed, generally shows different strength/hand-feel characteristics less suited to fine, premium woven fabric applications) doesn''t meet the specific quality/hand-feel requirements this premium end-use demands — illustrating that spinning technology selection depends on END-USE quality requirements, not production speed alone","Ring spinning is always cheaper than air-jet spinning regardless of production volume, making cost the only relevant decision factor in every scenario","Air-jet spinning can only produce yarn counts coarser than 30s Ne, making it technically incapable of this specific count regardless of any quality consideration"]'::jsonb, 'For premium woven shirting requiring the smooth, compact, high-strength, low-hairiness yarn character that ring spinning''s mechanism specifically produces, air-jet spinning''s yarn structure (which, despite high speed, generally shows different strength/hand-feel characteristics less suited to fine, premium woven fabric applications) doesn''t meet the specific quality/hand-feel requirements this premium end-use demands — illustrating that spinning technology selection depends on END-USE quality requirements, not production speed alone', 'This applies the general principle (already established for rotor-vs-ring selection) that spinning technology choice must match END-USE quality requirements, not simply chase production speed — premium, fine-count woven shirting specifically benefits from ring spinning''s compact, smooth, high-strength, low-hairiness yarn structure (directly connecting to the earlier compact-spinning and combed-yarn-quality discussions), and despite air-jet''s speed advantage, its yarn character is generally better suited to different, less quality-demanding applications, making ring spinning the technically correct choice for this specific premium application despite its lower production speed.', 3, '{"spinning-technology-selection"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A quality engineer compares two ring-spun yarn lots'' ''imperfections/km'' (thin places + thick places + neps combined, per USTER-style reporting) at three different sensitivity thresholds (e.g., thin places at -30%, -40%, -50% deviation from mean). Lot A shows LOW imperfections at the -30% (most sensitive) threshold but Lot B shows FEWER imperfections at the -50% (least sensitive) threshold. What does this pattern suggest about the two lots'' underlying defect SEVERITY distribution?', '["This pattern is impossible; imperfection counts at different sensitivity thresholds must always rank the two lots identically, making the scenario technically contradictory","Lot A has fewer SMALL/MODERATE irregularities (good at catching the more sensitive -30% threshold) but may have a higher proportion of genuinely SEVERE defects (worse at the -50% threshold, which only counts the most extreme irregularities) — while Lot B shows the reverse pattern (more small/moderate irregularities overall, but fewer of the most severe kind) — illustrating that a single aggregate imperfections/km number without threshold detail can mask meaningfully different underlying defect SEVERITY profiles between two yarn lots","Sensitivity threshold has no effect on imperfection counts, making any comparison across different thresholds meaningless by definition","A lot showing fewer defects at ANY threshold must automatically show fewer defects at EVERY threshold, contradicting the premise of this comparison scenario"]'::jsonb, 'Lot A has fewer SMALL/MODERATE irregularities (good at catching the more sensitive -30% threshold) but may have a higher proportion of genuinely SEVERE defects (worse at the -50% threshold, which only counts the most extreme irregularities) — while Lot B shows the reverse pattern (more small/moderate irregularities overall, but fewer of the most severe kind) — illustrating that a single aggregate imperfections/km number without threshold detail can mask meaningfully different underlying defect SEVERITY profiles between two yarn lots', 'This is an advanced statistical-distribution insight applied to yarn quality reporting — because different sensitivity thresholds count different SUBSETS of the full defect population (a -30% threshold catches all moderate-and-severe defects, while a -50% threshold catches ONLY the most extreme/severe ones), two lots can genuinely rank DIFFERENTLY depending on which threshold is examined, revealing that their underlying defect distributions have different SHAPES (one lot skewed toward many-but-mild irregularities, another toward fewer-but-more-severe ones) — a single blended ''imperfections/km'' number without this threshold-level detail can mask this genuinely important difference in defect severity profile, which matters because severe defects are typically far more visually/functionally disruptive in finished fabric than mild ones.', 3, '{"imperfection-threshold-distribution"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A combing machine''s ''detachment setting'' (the timing/distance parameter controlling when the detaching rollers pull fiber from the comb) is adjusted TIGHTER (shorter detachment distance) to try to reduce noil% and improve fiber yield. What is the most likely QUALITY tradeoff of this adjustment?', '["No tradeoff exists; tightening detachment setting always purely improves both yield AND quality simultaneously with zero downside","A tighter detachment setting reduces noil% (improving yield/reducing waste) but risks incorporating MORE short fiber and less-fully-combed material into the sliver (since less material is being rejected as noil), potentially INCREASING short fiber content and reducing the resulting yarn''s evenness/strength quality — directly trading yield improvement against fiber/yarn quality","Detachment setting only affects the combing machine''s production speed, with zero relationship to either noil percentage or resulting fiber/yarn quality","Tightening detachment setting always INCREASES noil percentage, the opposite of the stated adjustment''s intended and actual typical effect on noil%"]'::jsonb, 'A tighter detachment setting reduces noil% (improving yield/reducing waste) but risks incorporating MORE short fiber and less-fully-combed material into the sliver (since less material is being rejected as noil), potentially INCREASING short fiber content and reducing the resulting yarn''s evenness/strength quality — directly trading yield improvement against fiber/yarn quality', 'This directly extends the earlier combing-noil-percentage/cost-quality-tradeoff discussion with a specific machine-parameter mechanism — tightening detachment setting is a direct lever for reducing the AMOUNT of material rejected as noil (improving raw material yield/reducing cost), but since noil removal is precisely what eliminates short fiber and imperfectly-combed material, retaining more material via a tighter setting risks retaining more of exactly the short-fiber content that combing is meant to remove, directly trading yield/cost improvement against the resulting yarn''s quality (evenness, strength, smoothness) — a genuine, carefully-balanced machine-setting tradeoff mills must manage rather than simply maximizing yield.', 3, '{"combing-detachment-setting"}'::text[]),
('9dc3ade3-26ea-41fa-a83f-95a7440889b8', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A spinning technologist explains that a ring frame''s ''balloon control ring'' (a small ring positioned partway up the spindle, constraining the yarn balloon''s maximum radius) is used specifically at HIGHER spindle speeds. Why does the yarn balloon''s uncontrolled radius become a bigger problem at higher speeds?', '["Balloon radius has no relationship to spindle speed at all, making a speed-dependent control ring technically unnecessary at any speed","As spindle speed increases, centrifugal force on the rotating yarn increases (proportional to the square of rotational speed), causing the uncontrolled balloon to bulge to a LARGER radius — this larger, faster-moving balloon increases air drag/tension on the yarn AND risks physical contact with adjacent spindles/machine parts, so a balloon control ring becomes necessary specifically at higher speeds to constrain this growing radius and keep tension/clearance within safe limits","A larger balloon radius always REDUCES yarn tension, making a balloon control ring counterproductive at any speed since it would only interfere with this beneficial tension reduction","Balloon control rings are used exclusively at LOW spindle speeds, the opposite of their actual primary high-speed application"]'::jsonb, 'As spindle speed increases, centrifugal force on the rotating yarn increases (proportional to the square of rotational speed), causing the uncontrolled balloon to bulge to a LARGER radius — this larger, faster-moving balloon increases air drag/tension on the yarn AND risks physical contact with adjacent spindles/machine parts, so a balloon control ring becomes necessary specifically at higher speeds to constrain this growing radius and keep tension/clearance within safe limits', 'This connects to the earlier balloon-tension discussion with the underlying physics made explicit — centrifugal force scales with the SQUARE of rotational speed, so as mills push spindle speeds higher for productivity, the uncontrolled yarn balloon''s radius grows disproportionately, increasing both air-drag-driven tension (raising end-breakage risk) and the physical risk of the ballooning yarn contacting neighboring spindles/machine components — a balloon control ring physically constrains this radius, which is specifically why it becomes a necessary addition at higher speeds even though it wasn''t needed (and would even be counterproductive/unnecessary friction) at historically lower, more modest spindle speeds.', 3, '{"balloon-control-ring-physics"}'::text[]);
